 "findings": {
  "neg": "The group rule calls no NEG pair material (0 of 8). Per cell, R1 alone flags up to 7 of 20, too liberal to use alone.",
  "pos_sign": "L0 beats the single-donor table by 0.120-0.148 in three groups (A/MLP, B/partial_linear, B/partial_mlp), each material, and beats the four-donor table there by 0.083-0.098. In A/linear it does not (+0.000): in runs with sign -1 its precision is 0.260 against 0.54-0.59 for the other three groups, so it never learns the reversed relation.",
  "why_a_linear_fails": "Fit budget again: 300 Adam steps at learning rate 0.02 move a weight at most about 6 logits from a zero residual, and reversing the prior's +4 to -3 needs 7. Partial pooling stacks two linear stages and the MLP has more parameters, so both can reverse.",
  "per_cell_rules": "Unsuitable here. In POS-sign a cell's replicates have random signs, so the effect is large in about half and near zero in the rest: R1 needs 12 of 16 and rarely holds, and R2 (the early-shock slope) does not address a sign effect.",
  "pos_flip": "Forgetting tracks the flip in every group (post-shift +0.059 to +0.109 against L0). The four-donor table still beats L0 overall where it is material (ensembling)."
 },
